<section class="panel" aria-label="Grade 3 2021 source-linked Multiples of 100 lesson"><h2>Warm-up — Estimate the marching band</h2><p>View the 2021 marching-band photo with your teacher. Give estimates that are too low, about right and too high, and discuss whom you are counting. There is no automatic exact-crowd answer.</p><h2>Activity 1 — About 100? Close to 100?</h2><p>Work with a partner. Place each source school count in an <em>about 100/not about 100</em> table, then consider <em>close to 0 / close to 100 / close to 200 / set aside</em>. Different interpretations may be reasonable with justification.</p><table class="source-chart"><thead><tr><th>Place</th><th>People</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>playground</td><td>94</td></tr><tr><td>cafeteria</td><td>163</td></tr><tr><td>art room</td><td>36</td></tr><tr><td>library</td><td>13</td></tr><tr><td>classrooms</td><td>216</td></tr><tr><td>gymnasium</td><td>109</td></tr><tr><td>music room</td><td>52</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Important:</strong> 52 is numerically nearer to100 than0, but the original source says it might not seem close to either. The teacher reviews interpretations rather than assigning one fixed table key.</p><h2>Activity 2 — Number lines and the nearest hundred</h2><p>Your teacher assigns one original group A–E. On an independently made hundred-to-hundred number line, position and label your five values, then record each number's nearest multiple of100.</p><table class="source-chart"><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Five 2021 student source values</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">A</th><td>94, 36, 109, 163, 229</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">B</th><td>24, 52, 216, 11, 481</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">C</th><td>187, 135, 67, 83, 241</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">D</th><td>332, 154, 408, 296, 45</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">E</th><td>279, 205, 377, 449, 73</td></tr></tbody></table><div role="img" aria-label="An independently made hundred interval number line showing 364 between 300 and400, nearer400 than300" style="border:1px solid #8daeb7;padding:12px;border-radius:8px"><p>300 ————— 350 — 364 ——— 400</p><p>364 is64 from300 and36 from400, so400 is nearest.</p></div><p>Group members should compare their placements and reasons. The numeric Edulytics checks test the nearest hundred only; teachers must inspect the original diagram and explanations.</p><h2>Synthesis</h2><p>Explain why67 and135 both have nearest hundred100, and why449 is nearest400. Discuss how nearest-hundred distance differs from the everyday judgment that a number is “close to” another.</p><p><a href="https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-3/unit-3/lesson-13/lesson.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">2021 original teacher lesson</a> · <a href="https://cms-assets.illustrativemathematics.org/6eqxtlu604llirl45yjla15cuo27" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">2021 four-page student PDF</a>. The original band photograph and gated Cool-down are not reproduced.</p></section>